# tools/m/mycelium_puddle_witness.rish — the Puddle: a fleet places each world at exactly one host by a rule
# every node computes alike (r1, the berth crux).
#
# Season D (Kresfa & Mycelium) · Puddle fleet orchestration · rung r1 — the crux.
# The Mycelium season built how nodes AGREE — the Cord orders a DAG, the fold sums a supply, the Muster
# names a validator roll, the Warrant rotates it. Puddle builds the layer beneath: WHICH host runs WHICH
# world. Every fleet faces the trap the Warrant faced with authority — WHO decides the placement? A central
# scheduler is a single point of failure and of trust. The leaderless answer this tree keeps: placement is
# not DECIDED by anyone, it is DERIVED by everyone, to the same answer.
#
# The crux this witness proves is A WORLD BERTHS AT EXACTLY ONE HOST, AND EVERY NODE COMPUTES THE SAME
# BERTH: the same five worlds over three hosts, built in two different arrival orders, berth to the
# byte-identical placement (invariant under insertion order); capacity is honored, no world orphaned; an
# oversubscribed fleet refuses Oversubscribed whole, a fleet with no hosts refuses NoHosts. The method is
# highest weave-score — SHA-256(domain · world-id · host-pk) — a rendezvous/HRW concept studied clean-room,
# our own bounded Rye. Composes kumara + tally_copy public API only; demo host and world seeds — no key, no
# funds, no network, no world provisioned.
#
# Scope: active-designing/date/20260813/20260813-114651_puddle-fleet-placement-exploration.md
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/m/mycelium_puddle_witness.rish

say "mycelium-puddle: Language EN — a fleet trusts no scheduler; it trusts arithmetic, and arithmetic is the same in every hand: each world's host is derived from the roster alone, so two nodes reach one placement without ever speaking."
say "mycelium-puddle: Style Radiant — the Mycelium season built how nodes agree on order and supply; the Puddle builds the layer beneath, placing sovereign worlds onto hosts by a rule no one decides and everyone derives."
say "mycelium-puddle: Lens TAME — the berth law composes kumara + tally_copy public API; the fleet is bounded at max_hosts and max_worlds; the placement is a pure function of the fleet, invariant under arrival order; capacity is honored; refuses Oversubscribed and NoHosts whole; demo seeds only, no key held."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "mycelium/bin/puddle"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p mycelium/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build mycelium/puddle.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "mycelium-puddle: build failed"

let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "mycelium-puddle: selftest exited non-zero"
assert selftest.out contains "GREEN" else "mycelium-puddle: selftest did not speak GREEN"
assert selftest.out contains "a rule every node computes alike" else "mycelium-puddle: berth claim missing"
assert selftest.out contains "berth to a byte-identical placement whatever order the roster was built in" else "mycelium-puddle: arrival-order-independence claim missing"
assert selftest.out contains "placement is derived, never decided" else "mycelium-puddle: crux (derived not decided) claim missing"
assert selftest.out contains "Fleet placement without a scheduler stands" else "mycelium-puddle: closing claim missing"

# The refusal ledger a keeper reads — the whole-fleet refusals and the build refusals.
assert selftest.out contains "an oversubscribed fleet refuses Oversubscribed whole" else "mycelium-puddle: oversubscribed refusal missing"
assert selftest.out contains "a fleet with no hosts refuses NoHosts" else "mycelium-puddle: no-hosts refusal missing"
assert selftest.out contains "a duplicate host or world and a bad capacity or name each refuse" else "mycelium-puddle: build refusals missing"

# The rendered fleet a keeper reads — three hosts, five worlds.
assert selftest.out contains "fleet hosts=3 worlds=5" else "mycelium-puddle: rendered fleet (3 hosts, 5 worlds) missing"

say "mycelium-puddle: GREEN — placement is derived, never decided: a world's berth is a pure function of the fleet (highest weave-score over the roster), invariant under arrival order, so no scheduler is trusted; capacity is honored, no world orphaned, an oversubscribed fleet refuses whole. Fleet placement without a scheduler stands."
